WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.667
Grain remains the central safety rule.

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:06.297
A CTE can have a different grain from the source table.

00:00:06.297 --> 00:00:10.192
A window function usually preserves the current row grain.

00:00:10.192 --> 00:00:15.707
A later filter can reduce the number of rows without changing what one row represents.

00:00:15.707 --> 00:00:22.942
If you cannot explain the row meaning at each stage, advanced syntax can hide mistakes instead of solving

00:00:22.942 --> 00:00:29.627
them. Connect this idea to an operations team comparing each event with the previous and next event.

00:00:29.627 --> 00:00:35.347
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:35.347 --> 00:00:43.097
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:43.097 --> 00:00:48.772
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:48.772 --> 00:00:54.526
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
